<title>A place of certainty</title>
<category></category>
<!--
<description></description>
<img src="pix/cards/xxx.png" alt=""/>
-->
<body>

<p>My mother is 86, and she is declining. Her memory is failing. Things that
used to be easy for her now seem completely foreign. She was a programmer,
writing software before I could read, so it is very strange to see her
like this.</p>

<p>She no longer uses a computer. I mentioned some photos I found online, and
she asks if there's any way she can see them.  This is a new reality for me, but
is easier than a year or two ago when she still tried to be constantly online,
but couldn't because "the computer is haunted."</p>

<p>In many ways, she is following a similar path to her own mother, my
<a pref="text/victoria-olds.px">Grandma O</a>. Like her, my mom is accepting
of the changes in her relationship to the world.  She is able to laugh at it a
bit. But it will still be difficult, especially because we know it is a
progression that can only get worse.</p>

<p>We had a family gathering at my sister's house. My mom has been there many
times. It's become the usual location for big events. But this day she didn't
recognize it. I sat with my mom and sister over lunch and they were discussing
the dining room. It wasn't familiar to my mom. She wasn't upset about it, just
looked around and said, "no, I don't remember this."</p>

<p>We had mango slaw salad. My mom was enjoying it, but eating with her
hands.  I pointed to the fork on her plate and asked, "You don't like the fork?"
She looked at it as if we were discussing some unimportant detail of the
tablecloth, and kept eating the salad with her hands.  She wasn't bothered, just
calmly proceeded in her way.</p>

<p>At the end of the party, my step-mother Fumiko and my mom were getting ready
to go.  Fumiko had scheduled a ride-share car, so we went out to the street to
wait for it.  We brought out a chair for my mom to sit. The time for the car
came, but there was no car.  There were five of us there: me, my sister and
brother, my mother and her wife Fumiko.  Fumiko and my brother were trying to
figure out where the car was. They were looking through the app for information.
They re-read the email confirming the scheduled ride.  Should we keep waiting?
We could request a new ride. Would we be charged for the missed scheduled ride?
It was a whole thing, lots of discussion and questions.</p>

<p>Without any warning, my mom tried unsteadily to get up from her chair. Two of
us quickly intercepted her. The uneven pavement seemed particular treacherous
for her. We supported her arms to keep her steady.</p>

<p>"Mom, where are you trying to go?"</p>

<p>"I want a place of certainty. This place seems very uncertain."</p>

<p>In that moment, she was right: we were all uncertain. But I have to wonder if
she was also talking about her larger experience in a world that is less and
less understandable to her.</p>

<p>In the back of my mind, I wonder what my future holds. Seeing my grandmother
and now my mother failing in similar ways, I fear for myself.</p>

<p>I think we would all like a place of certainty. I know I would, but I also
know I am not going to get it soon.</p>


</body>
